      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "fa3fad2d-f1df-581b-8b0a-b2279bef8d7b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Widewuto, Bruteno, Grunau\n- Places: Prussia\n- Organizations: Teutonic Knights\n- Concepts: Prussian mythology, pagan religion, forced Christianization, polytheism, authenticity of legend\n- Events: Prussian Crusade\n\nFacts:\n- Widewuto, also called Viduutus, Vidvutus, Witowudi, and Waidewut, was a legendary king of the pagan Prussians who ruled with his elder brother Bruteno in the 6th century AD.\n- Bruteno was the high priest who ruled alongside Widewuto in the 6th century AD.\n- Prussian mythology was the polytheistic religion of the Old Prussians, the indigenous people of Prussia before the Prussian Crusade waged by the Teutonic Knights.\n- No sources document Prussian pagan religion before the forced Christianization in the 13th century.\n- It is unclear whether the legend of Widewuto and Bruteno was authentically Prussian or was created by Grunau, though Lithuanian researchers tend to support its authenticity.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c2db4458-fb9c-54d2-b05f-15fbbd134a62",
        "text": "Page: Widewuto. Sentence: Widewuto (also \"Viduutus\", \"Vidvutus\", \"Witowudi\", \"Waidewut\") was a legendary king of the pagan Prussians who ruled along with his elder brother, the high priest Bruteno in the 6th century AD.\nPage: Widewuto. Sentence: It is unclear whether the legend was authentically Prussian (i.e. recorded from Prussian mythology) or was created by Grunau (possibly inspired by Biblical Moses and Aaron), though Lithuanian researchers tend to support in its authenticity.\nPage: Prussian mythology. Sentence: The Prussian mythology was a polytheistic religion of the Old Prussians, indigenous peoples of Prussia before the Prussian Crusade waged by the Teutonic Knights.\nPage: Prussian mythology. Sentence: No sources document pagan religion before the forced Christianization in the 13th century.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
